 { When one side possesses an outside passed pawn, piece exchanges almost invariably favour him, as we saw several times in Chapter Two, starting with Fischer-Larsen. Here is one additional example.White has just played Rc2-f2, offering the exchange of rooks. } 1... { -KEY- } Rxf2 $2 ( { a) The ensuing bishop ending is bad for Black due to White's outside passed pawn - Lukacs; who instead recommends } 1... Rh8 $1 { , keeping the 'drawish' rooks on the board. Admittedly White could try to construct a scenario similar to the game by } 2. hxg5 Rg8 3. g6 Be8 4. Rf6 Ke7 5. Rf4 Bxg6 6. Rg4 { , but after } Bf7 ( { in contrast, } 6... Kf7 $2 { would lead the battle right into White's preferred endgame type - a pawn ending - after } 7. Bxg6 Rxg6 8. Rxg6 Kxg6 9. Kf2 { , when the outside passed pawn means an almost automatic win; } ) 
7. Rxg8 Bxg8 { , followed by ...Kf6 and ...e6-e5, Black's counterplay should be sufficient to hold the balance. } ) 
 ( { b) By the way, } 1... Rg8 $6 { is less precise due to } 2. h5 ) 
2. Kxf2 gxh4 3. gxh4 e5 $6 ( 3... Be8 { was preferable, trying to prevent h4-h5, when after } 4. Ke3 e5 5. Be2 { White keeps a clear, but not decisive advantage - Lukacs. } ) 
4. dxe5+ Kxe5 5. h5 $1 Kf6 6. h6 { Now that it has managed to get that far, the h-pawn will keep the enemy king occupied for the rest of the game. Meanwhile, White can use his king on the opposite flank. } a6 ( { After } 6... d4 7. Kf3 { , the d4-pawn just becomes exposed. } ) 
7. bxa6 ( { An alternative was } 7. b6 { , keeping more pawns on the board, but the text also works fine. } ) 
bxa6 8. Ke3 a5 9. Kd4 a4 10. a3 $1 { Of course - fixing the a-pawns on squares of the 'right' colour. } Bc6 11. Kc5 Bd7 ( { After } 11... Bb7 12. Kb5 { , the end is accelerated. } ) 
12. Kxd5 Be8 13. Kc5 Bf7 14. Kb4 Bb3 15. Kc3 Bd5 16. Bc2 Bc6 17. Kb4 Bd7 18. Bxa4 { From now on, White only needs to avoid a bishop sacrifice for the a-pawn. } Bf5 19. Bb5 ( { The game actually went } 19. Kc3 { , but later the position after 18...Bf5 was reached again. } ) 
Bc2 20. Kc3 Bd1 21. Be8 $1 { Zugzwang. } Be2 22. a4 Ba6 23. a5 Bb7 24. Kd4 Bc8 25. Kc5 Ba6 26. Kb6 Bc8 27. Bc6 Kg6 28. Bb7 
