 { As we saw in several examples in Chapter Four, the bishop pair is usually a great asset in any ending. Therefore the opponent is often happy to swap one of them, as in the following example.Of White's two bishops, the one on d2 is more valuable, so Olafsson prepares its elimination. } 1... { -KEY- } Nd3 $1 { A great square for the knight, where it denies the enemy rook the e1-square, among other things. } ( { Of course } 1... Be3 $2 { at once failed to } 2. Re1 ) 
2. Rf1 ( { On } 2. Rd1 { Black can reply in the same way. } ) 
Be3 $1 { Because of the attack on the f4-pawn, White can't avoid the exchange of his 'good' bishop. } 3. Bxe3 Rxe3 4. Bd1 f6 $1 { A small but powerful move, which prepares ...g6-g5 in order to create strongholds on e5 and f4. } 5. a3 Kg7 6. b4 $2 ( 6. Rf3 $1 { was preferable in order to swap the active enemy rook. Then Black should consider } Rxf3 ( { instead, } 6. b4 Ne1 7. Kf2 { doesn't really lead anywhere } ) 
7. Kxf3 g5 $1 { with a superior minor piece ending thanks to White's multiple weaknesses. } ) 
g5 $1 7. fxg5 fxg5 8. Bf3 $2 { A tactical oversight in a difficult position. } Ne1+ $1 9. Kf2 Rxf3+ 10. Kxe1 Rxf1+ 11. Kxf1 Kf6 { Seeing that the d5-pawn soon falls, White resigned. } 
 0-1

