 { On the other hand, sometimes it is the side with the bishop pair who deliberately swaps one of them in exchange for another advantage. We already witnessed this idea in Hamdouchi-Bricard and Spassky-Cramling, and here are two more.Black's position appears to be solid, but White found a simple path to victory. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxd5 $1 exd5 2. Bxd6 $1 Kxd6 { In this pawn ending, several factors speak in White's favour: a more active king, a sounder pawn structure, and a greater reserve of waiting moves } 3. Kd4 $2 { Surprisingly enough, this natural move allows a saving opportunity for Black. } ( 3. a4 $1 { first was called for, gaining a vital piece of space before the opponent prevents this idea by ...b7-b6 and ...a6-a5. After } b6 { ( } Ke5 $6 4. a5 { leaves Black short of waiting moves) } 4. a5 $1 bxa5 5. bxa5 Kc5 { (or } Ke5 6. f3 $1 Ke6 7. Kd4 Kd6 8. f4 { and the opposition decides) } 6. f3 $1 { -preparing to create a passed pawn on the e-file- } Kb5 7. Kd4 Kxa5 8. Kc5 $1 { (this exquisite non-capture is the key to success; instead } 8. Kxd5 $2 { fails to } Kb6 $1 { approaching the e-pawn while unleashing the a-pawn) } Ka4 9. Kxd5 a5 10. e4 { , Black's king turns out to be exposed to skewers once the queen ending is reached: } fxe4 11. fxe4 Kb3 { (11...Kb4 runs into checks on b8-e5/a8 in the end) } 12. e5 a4 13. e6 a3 14. e7 a2 15. e8=Q $11 a1=Q $11 16. Qe3 $1 Kc2 17. Qe2 Kb3 { (or } Kc3 18. Qe5 { ) } 18. Qc4 { and White manages to trade queens on the next move, reaching an easily won pawn endgame. An impressive variation, which we definitely can't blame Ehlvest for missing over the board! Now back to the game. } ) 
b6 4. a4 Ke6 $2 ( 4... a5 $1 { is the reason why 3.Kd4 was wrong. After } 5. bxa5 bxa5 6. f4 Kc6 7. Ke5 Kc5 { , Black starts a counterattack at the e3-pawn: } 8. Kf6 Kc4 9. Kxg6 Kd3 { and the d-pawn soon queens. Once there, it will be able to capture the a4-pawn, with drawish consequences, as observed by FSU70. } ) 
5. f3 Kd6 6. a5 $1 ( { The game actually saw some manoeuvring at this point: } 6. Kc3 Ke5 7. Kd3 Ke6 8. Kc3 Ke5 9. Kd3 Ke6 10. Kd4 Kd6 { and only now } 11. a5 $1 ) 
bxa5 ( { On } 6... b5 { White takes the opposition by } 7. f4 { with an easy win. } ) 
