 { White is better with his bishop pair, but having observed a very favourable bishop ending on the horizon, Kuzubov gives one of them away. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxe6 $1 ( { Objectively speaking, } 1. Ke4 { was better, for instance, if two chess engines were playing against each other. However, in practical terms, the game continuation is quite strong, since Black is faced with a difficult defensive task over the board. } ) 
Kxe6 2. Ke4 { Black is forced to put another pawn on a dark square. } g5 3. hxg5 ( 3. h5 $6 { is less convincing due to } b5 $1 { and Black secures a healthy queenside structure. } ) 
hxg5 4. a4 $1 { Preparing b4-b5 in order to fix more pawns. By now the virtues of White's initial decision become evident: in this bishop ending, the fact that all the enemy pawns finish up on squares of the 'wrong' colour will make Black's task very difficult. } ( { By the way, the immediate } 4. b5 { was also okay. } ) 
Bc3 ( { Or } 4... a6 5. b5 $1 { with Ba3 next. } ) 
5. b5 Bf6 6. Bd2 { Kuzubov starts looking for a zugzwang. } Bd8 7. Bc3 Bc7 8. Bd4 Bb8 9. f3 Bc7 10. Bc3 Bb8 ( { If } 10... Bd8 { , then } 11. Be5 ) 
11. a5 $1 { This advance creates another target on b6. } Bc7 ( { After } 11... bxa5 12. Bxa5 { , the threat of 13.Bd8 decides. } ) 
12. axb6 ( { But not } 12. a6 $6 Bb8 13. Bd4 Bc7 ) 
axb6 $2 ( 12... Bxb6 $1 { was called for, making it harder for White to get at Black's queenside pawn. For instance, } 13. Be5 Bc5 14. Bc7 Bb6 $1 { (but not } Kd7 15. Ba5 $1 Ke6 16. Bd8 { etc.) } 15. Bxb6 axb6 16. Kd4 Kd6 { , keeping the enemy king at bay. } ) 
13. Bd4 Bd8 14. Be5 $1 { Zugzwang. } Be7 15. Bc7 ( { In view of } 15. Bc7 Bc5 16. Bd8 { , Black resigned. } ) 

