 { Needless to say, the exchange of queens has a particular effect on the battle. From my experience, swapping one's active queen is quite a common endgame mistake in practice, and it seems to appear with more frequency when facing stronger players, perhaps because of a false belief that the absence of queens makes a peaceful result more probable. Here is one example.Black has a slight initiative, but there are no obvious weaknesses in White's camp just yet. } 1. { -KEY- } Qxg6+ $2 { A bad choice, as the queen had an active future on c6 or c7. } ( { a) In effect, } 1. Qc6 $1 { practically forced a draw since } Qd3 2. Kg2 Ne4 3. Qxe6 Qc2 { is met by } 4. Qf5 g6 5. Ng5 $1 fxg5 6. Qf7 { with perpetual. } ) 
 ( { b) Equally possible was } 1. Qc7 { ; e.g. } Qd3 2. Kg2 Ne4 3. Qf7 Kh6 4. Qe8 { with a perpetual again coming up. } ) 
Kxg6 2. Nd2 ( { Perhaps } 2. Ng1 $5 Kf5 3. Ne2 g5 4. f3 { was more tenacious - Akesson. } ) 
Kf5 { Evidently, such a king manoeuvre would have been a bit more difficult to realize in the presence of queens. } 3. f3 g5 $1 { Preparing ...g5-g4 to soften up the e4-square. } 4. Ke2 $2 { Very passive. } ( { It made sense to clear some space for the defence by } 4. hxg5 fxg5 5. Ke2 { ; e.g. } g4 ( { or } 5. Ke2 h4 6. Kf2 ) 
6. b4 $5 { , when } gxf3 7. Kxf3 Ne4 $6 { leads nowhere after } 8. Nxe4 dxe4 9. Kg2 Kg4 10. b5 { etc. } ) 
g4 5. f4 ( { An ugly move; but after } 5. b4 gxf3 6. Kxf3 Ne4 { , White would be in trouble anyway; e.g. } 7. Nxe4 $2 dxe4 8. Kg2 Kg4 9. b5 { and, in contrast to the previous note, here Black has a decisive spare tempo in } f5 ) 
Ne4 $1 6. Nf1 ( { In view of } 6. Nxe4 Kxe4 7. b4 a6 { etc, White is forced to displace his knight for the rest of the game. } ) 
a6 7. Kd3 { Black has achieved a great deal in just seven moves, but how to progress further? } a5 $1 { With the strong plan of a king march to c6, followed by ...b6-b5 to open up a new front. Meanwhile, White can only wait. } 8. Ke2 Kg6 9. Kd3 Kf7 10. Kc2 Ke7 11. Kd3 Kd6 12. Kc2 Kc6 13. Kd3 b5 14. Kc2 ( { If } 14. axb5 Kxb5 15. Kc2 { , then } Kb4 16. Kb2 f5 { with a decisive zugzwang - Akesson. } ) 
bxa4 15. bxa4 Kd6 $1 { The king returns, now that a new weakness has been established on a4. } 16. Kd3 ( { Or } 16. Kb2 e5 17. Kb3 Ke6 18. Kc2 Nd6 $1 19. Kd3 Nc4 { - Akesson, when } 20. Kc2 exd4 21. exd4 Kf5 { is similar to the game. } ) 
e5 { Apart from clearing the e6-f5 route for the king, this advance will help Black soften up the d4-pawn later on. } 17. Kc2 Ke6 18. Kd3 Kf5 { Zugzwang. } 19. Kc2 ( { a) After } 19. Ke2 Nc3 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 19. dxe5 fxe5 20. fxe5 Kxe5 21. Kc2 Nc5 { , Black also wins. } ) 
exf4 ( { A different move order with } 19... exd4 { also works, as noticed by gongbaojiding. } ) 
20. exf4 Nf2 $1 { Clearing the way for his majesty. } 21. Kd2 Ke4 22. Ke2 Nh1 23. Nd2+ Kxd4 24. Nb3+ Kc3 
 0-1

